## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 31)
**Ref:** Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — tsDetect Smell #1

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — the if/else means one branch is never verified for a given run
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Fix — split into two parameterised tests:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardAmount_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumAmount_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is invisible per run — a regression in that branch will never be caught.

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)
**Ref:** Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — tsDetect Smell #4

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — no assertion; passes even if ProcessOrder returns garbage
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// ...that's it
```

**Fix — assert on the expected outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_SingleItem_ReturnsProcessedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(result.TotalAmount > 0, "Total should be positive");
}
```
**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage metrics look healthy but nothing is verified.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test
**Test:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 98)
**Ref:** Peruma et al., ESEC/FSE 2020 — tsDetect Smell #3; Camara et al. SAST 2021 (flakiness correlation)

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — arbitrary 2-second delay, classic source of flaky failures
Thread.Sleep(2000);
```

**Fix — use a polling helper or make the async method awaitable:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
